catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ 06 — failing, and recovering.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D-71): aborting is not a control-flow primitive. `Fail` is an
\ effect with one operation, `try`/`catch` is its handler, and `!Fail` in a
\ row means "this may not return normally" the same way `!IO` means "this may
\ print". It composes with everything in demos 02–05 because it is not
\ special.
\
\ This is also the demo with the most honest gaps in it, and they are all one
\ gap: failure carries no value yet.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- Unhandled, `fail` escapes to the top like any other operation.
catcat> fail
unhandled: fail escaped with no handler in scope
catcat> \ --- `try { … } catch { … }` discharges it. The row of the whole expression
\ has no `!Fail` in it.
catcat> define validate ( $n:i64 -- i64 !Fail ) { $n 0 < if { } then { fail 0 } else { $n } endif }
defined validate ( i64 -- i64 !Fail )
catcat> try { 7 validate } catch { 0 } show print " " print
try { -7 validate } catch { 0 } show print "\n" print
7 0
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- Named locals are in scope in the try block, so the ordinary wrapper is
\ ordinary to write:
catcat> define or_zero ( $n:i64 -- i64 ) { try { $n validate } catch { 0 } }
defined or_zero ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> 7 or_zero show print " " print
-7 or_zero show print "\n" print
7 0
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ A try block still runs on its OWN stack — it cannot reach anonymous values
\ that were already there — so what makes this work is that each local the
\ block reads is COPIED into it before the block runs. The residual shows the
\ whole mechanism: pick the local in, consume the copy, drop the original.
catcat> locate or_zero
define or_zero ( i64 -- i64 ) {
  pick.0 try { roll.0 validate } catch { 0 } roll.1 pop
}
catcat> \ Copies, not moves, and that is forced rather than chosen: an abort cuts the
\ stack back past whatever the block was given, so a moved local would be
\ destroyed on a path nobody wrote. The price is that the type must be `Copy`.
catcat> define linear ( $b:Box[i64] -- i64 ) { try { 1 $b pop } catch { 0 } }
error: $b has a non-copyable type and cannot be read inside a try block; the block runs on its own stack, so its locals are copied into it
catcat> \ What is NOT in scope is a local in the `catch` block, and for the same reason
\ the copies exist: by the time catch runs, the stack has been cut back.
catcat> define incatch ( $n:i64 -- i64 ) { try { 1 } catch { $n } }
error: $n is not in scope in a catch block: an abort cuts the stack back before catch runs, so it receives nothing. Read the local in the try block instead
catcat> \ And an anonymous value on the enclosing stack is still out of reach — only
\ named locals are copied in, because only they can be identified.
catcat> define reaches ( i64 -- i64 ) { try { 1 + } catch { 0 } }
error: stack underflow calling +: it needs 2 inputs
catcat> \ Look at `validate`'s body: `fail 0`. `fail` is `( -- !Fail )`, so it cannot
\ stand where a value is expected, and the two branches of an `if` must agree
\ on what they leave behind. The `0` is dead — nothing runs after `fail` — and
\ it is there only to make the branch types line up. What is wanted is a `fail`
\ at the empty type, `( -- #A !Fail )`; the core already HAS the empty type
\ (`TSum []`, uninhabited) and generics now exist, so this is a small change
\ that has not been made. See U01 §9.
catcat> \ --- Everything the try block built is discarded, so `catch` takes no inputs
\ and must leave what the try block would have left.
catcat> try { 1 2 3 + + fail } catch { -1 } show print "\n" print
-1
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- Disagreeing about that is what gets rejected:
catcat> try { 42 } catch { "nope" }
error: try: the catch block must leave the same stack as the try block
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Composing
\ ======================================================================
catcat> effect Log { declare say ( str -- ) }
effect Log say
catcat> \ A word that both logs and may fail carries both:
catcat> define risky ( $n:i64 -- i64 !Log !Fail ) {
  "  checking\n" say
  $n 100 < if { } then { $n } else { "  too big\n" say fail 0 } endif
}
defined risky ( i64 -- i64 !Log !Fail )
catcat> \ Handle them in either order — they are independent rows, not a monad stack
\ that has to be assembled in the right sequence.
catcat> define small ( -- i64 ) {
  handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { print } } { try { 42 risky } catch { -1 } }
}
defined small ( -- i64 !IO )
catcat> define big ( -- i64 ) {
  handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { print } } { try { 999 risky } catch { -1 } }
}
defined big ( -- i64 !IO )
catcat> "(a) 42:\n" print small show print "\n" print
"(b) 999:\n" print big show print "\n" print
(a) 42:
  checking
42
(b) 999:
  checking
  too big
-1
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Note that (b) printed "checking" and "too big" before failing. The log
\ survives the abort because the handler is OUTSIDE the `try`: the abort cuts
\ the stack back, not the effects already performed. Put the handler inside
\ and the captured log would be discarded with everything else.
catcat> \ --- And with the Dictionary: `validate` is an ordinary word, so demo 03's
\ rebinding replaces the whole failing policy from outside.
catcat> define permissive ( i64 -- i64 ) { }
defined permissive ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> define lenient ( -- i64 ) { with { validate permissive } { try { -7 validate } catch { 0 } } }
defined lenient ( -- i64 )
catcat> lenient show print "\n" print
-7
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ The gaps, named
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ 1. `fail` takes no payload and `catch` receives none, so a failure cannot
\    say what went wrong. That is why the prelude's `parse` returns 0 on bad
\    input and `getenv` returns "" when unset: their honest signatures are
\    `( str -- i64 !Fail )` and `( str -- str !Fail )`, and the reason they
\    still return sentinels is not that failure is unavailable but that a
\    caller who WANTS the sentinel has no way to ask for it back.
catcat> "not a number" parse show print "\n" print
0
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ 2. `catch` receives nothing, so a recovery cannot see what was being tried.
\    That is the same missing piece as (1): the fix both want is `catch` taking
\    a typed payload, which needs the abort to carry a value. Tracked as Q-22
\    in NOTES/N02_Open_Questions.md.
\
\ 3. An abort discards values without consulting their capabilities, so a
\    linear value in flight when a `fail` fires is leaked rather than
\    released. Tracked as Q-17 in NOTES/N02_Open_Questions.md. Nothing
\    demonstrates it yet, because no surface word constructs a `Box`.
